[section:dycors Dynamic Coordinate Search with Radial Basis Functions]

[heading Synopsis]

``
    #include <boost/math/optimization/dycors.hpp>

    namespace boost::math::optimization {

    template <typename ArgumentContainer> struct dycors_parameters {
        using Real = typename ArgumentContainer::value_type;
        ArgumentContainer lower_bounds;
        ArgumentContainer upper_bounds;
        ArgumentContainer const * initial_guess = nullptr;
        size_t max_function_calls = 0;
        size_t initial_points = 0;
        size_t candidates = 0;
    };

    template <typename ArgumentContainer>
    void validate_dycors_parameters(dycors_parameters<ArgumentContainer> const &params);

    template <typename ArgumentContainer, class Func, class URBG>
    ArgumentContainer dycors(const Func cost_function,
                             dycors_parameters<ArgumentContainer> const &params,
                             URBG &gen,
                             std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer> target_value
                               = std::numeric_limits<std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>>::quiet_NaN(),
                             std::atomic<bool> *cancellation = nullptr,
                             std::atomic<std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>> *current_minimum_cost = nullptr,
                             std::vector<std::pair<ArgumentContainer, std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>>> *queries = nullptr);

    } // namespaces
``

The `dycors` function searches for a global minimum of a function on a box with DYCORS (DYnamic COordinate search using Response Surface models),
the method of Regis and Shoemaker, in its DYCORS-LMSRBF form.
Like [link math_toolkit.turbo TuRBO], it is meant for expensive functions and budgets of a few hundred to a few thousand calls,
but it was designed for many dimensions, from tens to hundreds.

DYCORS fits a cubic radial basis function surrogate with a linear tail, s(/x/) = [sum][sub /k/] λ[sub /k/] |/x/ - /x/[sub /k/]|[super 3] + /a/[sub 0] + /a/[super T] /x/,
to all the points it has evaluated, starting from a symmetric Latin hypercube design of 2(/d/ + 1) points.
Each iteration perturbs the best point found so far into a few thousand candidate points, and evaluates the candidate that is best by a weighted combination of
its (scaled) surrogate value and its (scaled) distance to the points already evaluated, with the weight on the surrogate cycling through 0.3, 0.5, 0.8 and 0.95.
The candidates differ from the best point in only some of their coordinates: each coordinate is perturbed with probability

[expression /p/(/n/) = min(20\/ /d/, 1) (1 - ln(/n/ - /n/[sub 0] + 1) \/ ln(/N/ - /n/[sub 0]))]

where /n/ is the number of calls so far, /n/[sub 0] the size of the initial design and /N/ the budget, and at least one coordinate is always perturbed.
In high dimensions, this keeps the search local even with large steps, and it focuses on fewer coordinates as the budget runs out.
The perturbations are normal with standard deviation σ, in units of the sides of the box.
σ starts at 0.2, doubles after three improvements in a row, and halves (down to 0.2 × 2[super -6]) after max(/d/, 5) calls in a row that do not improve on the best value.

DYCORS is a local search with a global model: it has no restarts, and it converges to a local minimum.
In the comparison below, in 30 dimensions it is about as good as TuRBO on Ackley, a little better on Rastrigin and a little worse on Levy and Rosenbrock, at a thirtieth of the cost per call or less,
and much better on Ackley and Rosenbrock in 10 dimensions, but it stops in a local minimum of Rastrigin and Levy in 10 dimensions where TuRBO, which restarts, keeps improving.
Multilevel coordinate search, which is deterministic and spends a microsecond per call, is competitive with both at this budget,
helped on Levy and Rosenbrock by its first point, the centre of the box, which is already a good one.
For cheap functions, multilevel coordinate search, differential evolution, jSO or CMA-ES are better choices.

[heading Parameters]

* `lower_bounds`: A container representing the lower bounds of the optimization space along each dimension.
    The `.size()` of the bounds should return the dimension of the problem.
* `upper_bounds`: A container representing the upper bounds of the optimization space along each dimension.
    It should have the same size as `lower_bounds`, and each element must be strictly greater than the corresponding element of `lower_bounds`.
* `initial_guess`: An optional point in the box. It is the first function call, exactly as given, in addition to the initial design.
* `max_function_calls`: The budget /N/. Defaults to 100 times the dimension. The probability /p/(/n/) above depends on it, so it is part of the algorithm, not just a stopping criterion.
* `initial_points`: The number /n/[sub 0] of points of the symmetric Latin hypercube design that starts the search. Defaults to 2(/d/ + 1). It must be at least 2/d/, and at most `max_function_calls` if that is set.
* `candidates`: The number of candidate points per iteration. Defaults to min(100/d/, 5000).

`validate_dycors_parameters` throws a `std::domain_error` if the bounds or initial guess are invalid,
and a `std::invalid_argument` if `initial_points` is out of range.
`dycors` calls it before the first function evaluation.

[heading The function]

``
template <typename ArgumentContainer, class Func, class URBG>
ArgumentContainer dycors(const Func cost_function,
                         dycors_parameters<ArgumentContainer> const &params,
                         URBG &gen,
                         std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer> target_value
                           = std::numeric_limits<std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>>::quiet_NaN(),
                         std::atomic<bool> *cancellation = nullptr,
                         std::atomic<std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>> *current_minimum_cost = nullptr,
                         std::vector<std::pair<ArgumentContainer, std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>>> *queries = nullptr)
``

Parameters:

* `cost_function`: The cost function to be minimized. A NaN return value is treated as +[infin].
* `params`: The parameters to the algorithm as described above.
* `gen`: A uniform random bit generator, like `std::mt19937_64`.
* `target_value`: An optional value that, if reached, stops the optimization.
    This is the most robust way to terminate the calculation, but in most cases the optimal value of the cost function is unknown.
        If it is, use it! Physical considerations can often be used to find optimal values for cost functions.
* `cancellation`: An optional atomic boolean to allow the user to stop the computation and gracefully return the best result found up to that point.
    It is checked before every function call.
* `current_minimum_cost`: An optional atomic variable to store the current minimum cost during optimization.
    This allows developers to (e.g.) plot the progress of the minimization over time and in conjunction with the `cancellation` argument allow halting the computation when the progress stagnates.
* `queries`: An optional vector to store intermediate results during optimization.
    This is useful for debugging and perhaps volume rendering of the objective function after the calculation is complete.

Returns:

The `ArgumentContainer` corresponding to the minimum cost found by the optimization.
If the computation is cancelled before the first function call, it is the midpoint of the box (or the initial guess).

[heading Termination]

As in the paper, there is no convergence criterion.
The computation stops when the `target_value` is reached, when `max_function_calls` calls have been made, or when `cancellation` is set.
These are checked before every call.

[heading Cost]

Each iteration solves for the surrogate's coefficients in O(/N/[super 2]) operations, where /N/ is the number of points evaluated,
draws the /M/ candidates in O(/Md/), and evaluates the surrogate and the distances at them in O(/MNk/),
where /k/ is the average number of coordinates a candidate perturbs: the squared distances from the best point are computed once per iteration,
and a candidate only corrects them in its perturbed coordinates.
With the defaults, on an Apple M1 Pro core, a call costs on average 0.36 ms in 10 dimensions, 1.4 ms in 30 dimensions and 3.1 ms in 100 dimensions over a budget of 500 calls,
on a cheap function (`reporting/performance/dycors_performance.cpp`). (TuRBO, with its defaults, costs 45 ms and 90 ms per call in 10 and 30 dimensions.)
The points, the factor of the interpolation system and the candidates take O(/N/[super 2] + (/N/ + /M/)/d/) memory, with /N/ the budget.
All of the linear algebra is hand-rolled, and done in double precision whatever the `Real` type of the arguments.

[heading Implementation notes]

The algorithm and its parameters follow Section 3 of the paper, except for these deliberate differences:

* The box is scaled to the unit cube, so that the step sizes are in units of each side of the box rather than of its shortest side.
* As in Regis and Shoemaker (2007), the function values above their median are replaced by the median when the surrogate is fitted,
    which keeps a few large values from making it oscillate.
* σ never exceeds 1, the side of the unit cube; the paper has no cap.
* A success, for the counters, is an improvement on the best value by more than 10[super -3] times its magnitude, as in TuRBO; the paper counts any decrease.
* The probability /p/(/n/) never falls below 1\/ /d/.

The details that the paper leaves open are:

* Values that are +[infin] (or NaN) are replaced, when the surrogate is fitted, by the largest finite value, or by 0 if there is none.
* As in the paper, candidates that leave the box are reflected at its faces until they are inside.
* The interpolation system of a cubic radial basis function with a linear tail is indefinite.
    It is reduced to a positive definite one on all but /d/ + 1 of the points (a set that determines linear polynomials, chosen from the initial design),
    whose Cholesky factor grows by one row for each new point. A new point that is numerically a repeat of one already in the surrogate is left out of it.
* The initial guess, if any, is evaluated before the initial design, which is redrawn until it determines a linear polynomial;
    /n/[sub 0] in /p/(/n/) counts both.

Only DYCORS-LMSRBF is implemented, not DYCORS-DDS, and only one point is evaluated per iteration.

[h4:examples Examples]

``
#include <boost/math/optimization/dycors.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <iostream>
#include <random>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    using boost::math::optimization::dycors;
    using boost::math::optimization::dycors_parameters;
    using ArgType = std::vector<double>;
    // The Ackley function in 10 dimensions, with its minimum 0 at the origin:
    auto ackley = [](ArgType const & x) {
        double const pi = 3.14159265358979323846;
        double a = 0;
        double b = 0;
        for (double t : x) {
            a += t*t;
            b += std::cos(2*pi*t);
        }
        return -20*std::exp(-0.2*std::sqrt(a/10)) - std::exp(b/10) + 20 + std::exp(1.0);
    };
    dycors_parameters<ArgType> params;
    params.lower_bounds.assign(10, -15);
    params.upper_bounds.assign(10, 20);
    params.max_function_calls = 500;
    std::mt19937_64 gen(42);
    auto x = dycors(ackley, params, gen);
    std::cout << "f(x) = " << ackley(x) << "\n";
}
``

With seed 42 the value is 5.4×10[super -4]. Over 30 other seeds, 23 runs reached values between 1.3×10[super -4] and 2.0×10[super -3], and the other 7 stopped in local minima with values between 1.6 and 2.6:
DYCORS has no restarts, so a run that has settled in a local minimum stays there.

[h4:comparison Comparison]

The median, over 20 seeds, of the best value found in 500 calls by DYCORS, TuRBO, multilevel coordinate search (MCS, which is deterministic, so one run), CMA-ES and random search, all with their default parameters,
on the Ackley (\[-15, 20\][super /d/]), Rastrigin (\[-4, 5\][super /d/]), Rosenbrock (\[-5, 10\][super /d/]) and Levy (\[-5, 5\][super /d/]) functions, whose minimum is 0:

[table
[[Function] [Dimension] [DYCORS] [TuRBO] [MCS] [CMA-ES] [random search]]
[[Ackley] [10] [3.3×10[super -4]] [1.2] [1.7×10[super -7]] [2.9] [15]]
[[Rastrigin] [10] [30] [20] [40] [59] [87]]
[[Rosenbrock] [10] [7.1] [53] [0.22] [87] [3.6×10[super 4]]]
[[Levy] [10] [1.3] [0.50] [0.72] [0.27] [5.1]]
[[Ackley] [30] [3.2] [3.2] [2.5] [9.8] [17]]
[[Rastrigin] [30] [97] [106] [144] [284] [366]]
[[Rosenbrock] [30] [9.8×10[super 2]] [4.3×10[super 2]] [8.1×10[super 3]] [2.1×10[super 4]] [9.0×10[super 5]]]
[[Levy] [30] [3.1] [2.3] [2.7] [8.2] [35]]
]

[h4:references References]

* R. G. Regis and C. A. Shoemaker, ['Combining radial basis function surrogates and dynamic coordinate search in high-dimensional expensive black-box optimization.] Engineering Optimization 45(5), 529--555 (2013). [@https://doi.org/10.1080/0305215X.2012.687731 doi:10.1080/0305215X.2012.687731].
* R. G. Regis and C. A. Shoemaker, ['A stochastic radial basis function method for the global optimization of expensive functions.] INFORMS Journal on Computing 19(4), 497--509 (2007).
* K. Q. Ye, W. Li and A. Sudjianto, ['Algorithmic construction of optimal symmetric Latin hypercube designs.] Journal of Statistical Planning and Inference 90(1), 145--159 (2000).

